<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kolkhoz</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as kolkhozy, collective farm, kolkhoznik</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A kolkhoz was a collective farm in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>, the main form of Soviet agriculture alongside the state farms, or sovkhozes. The first were organised soon after 1918, and after forced collectivisation the agricultural artel became the only form by 1938. Legally a cooperative, in practice it was run by district and provincial authorities, and membership was neither voluntary nor freely left. Members, kolkhozniki, were paid by labour days, often in little or no cash, while the farm sold its produce to the state at very low fixed prices, a major source of state income. Private plots of about one acre produced a large share of output. Their number declined rapidly after 1992.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kolkhoz.jpg" alt="Kolkhoz" width="330" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Неизвестный художник (не указан в выходных данных плаката; отсутствует авторская монограмма или подпись) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Колхозник,_читай_книгу!_Книга_поможет_выполнить_план_второй_большевистской_весны.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The word kolkhoz is a contraction of the Russian for 'collective farm'. Soon after the first Soviet state farms were created in 1918 the first collective farms were organised. There were three main types: partnerships for joint cultivation, in which only labour was shared; agricultural artels, the commonest form, which pooled land, equipment and livestock but let members keep a plot and small livestock for themselves; and agricultural communes, the most radical form, in which even housing and everyday consumption were shared. By 1938, as a result of complete collectivisation, the artel was the only official form.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Standard Charter, which had the force of law from the early 1930s, described the kolkhoz as a voluntary cooperative managed by its members. In practice the general assembly and elected management only approved the plans and targets of district and provincial authorities, and members had no right to leave freely or to take their share of assets with them. By July 1929 a large kolkhoz of 200 to 400 households was usually divided into work units of 15 to 30 households, headed by a brigade leader.</p><p>A member, a kolkhoznik, received a share of the produce according to the number of labour days worked, and many farms paid no cash. The farm had to sell its grain to the state at fixed prices. In 1948 the government charged wholesalers 335 rubles for 100 kilograms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a> and paid the kolkhoz roughly 8. The difference was a major source of state income, used to buy foreign machinery for industrialisation. Private plots of about one acre were only 3.9 percent of the sown land in 1938, yet in 1937 they gave 21.5 percent of gross agricultural output.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dissolution-of-the-soviet-union">dissolution of the Soviet Union</a> in December 1991 the number of kolkhozes and sovkhozes declined rapidly from 1992, and other corporate forms gained ground. Surveys in the 1990s found that many of the new corporate farms still behaved like the old kolkhozes. In the Transcaucasian and Central Asian states the kolkhoz almost disappeared. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkmenistan">Turkmenistan</a> reorganised its kolkhozes into peasant associations by decree in 1995, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbekistan">Uzbekistan</a> renamed them shirkats in its Land Code of 1998.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kolkhoz” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
